{% block titre %}The basics of the notation{% endblock %}
{% block meta_description %}Staff, clefs and the basic neumes of Gregorian chant (punctum, pes, clivis, torculus…) explained with simple diagrams.{% endblock %}

{% block contenu %}
<h1>The basics&nbsp;: learning to see a neume</h1>
<p class="chapeau">
    Before opening the <em>Graduale Triplex</em>, let us set up a few simple landmarks.
    By the end of this page you will recognise the Gregorian staff, its clef, and about a
    dozen fundamental neumes.
</p>

<h2>1. The four-line staff</h2>
<p class="lettrine">
    Our modern staff has five lines. Gregorian chant, by contrast, is written on a staff of
    only <strong>four lines</strong> (the <span class="mot-latin">tetragram</span>). That is
    enough, because Gregorian melodies move within a fairly narrow range. As today, the
    <em>higher</em> a sign sits on the staff, the <em>higher</em> the sound&nbsp;; the
    <em>lower</em> it sits, the <em>lower</em> the sound. One peculiarity worth knowing&nbsp;:
    <strong>the clef can sit on different lines</strong> — it is not fixed once and for all as
    in modern music, and it sometimes even changes in the middle of a piece.
</p>

<div class="encadre def">
    <span class="etiquette">Definition — The clef</span>
    <p>
        A <strong>clef</strong> is a marker placed at the start of a line that names one of
        the lines. In chant, two clefs are mainly used&nbsp;: the
        <strong><em>C</em> clef</strong> (do) <img src="{{ asset('assets/img/cle-ut.png') }}" alt="C clef" class="icone-cle">
        and the <strong><em>F</em> clef</strong> <img src="{{ asset('assets/img/cle-fa.png') }}" alt="F clef" class="icone-cle">.
        They do not fix an absolute pitch
        (the choir adjusts the pitch to its voices), but they show where the semitones fall,
        which is enough to sing in tune.
    </p>
</div>

<h2>2. The basic note&nbsp;: the punctum</h2>
<p>
    In square notation the commonest single note is the
    <strong><span class="mot-latin">punctum</span></strong> — literally a «&nbsp;dot&nbsp;»,
    drawn as a small square. A variant, the <strong><span class="mot-latin">virga</span></strong>
    («&nbsp;rod&nbsp;»), is a square extended by a small stem&nbsp;; it often notes a higher
    note. When reading, <em>punctum</em> and <em>virga</em> are both worth a single note.
</p>

