<h2>5. Two signs that change the colour of the chant</h2>
<p>
    Some small signs do not change the note, but the <em>way</em> it is sung.
    Here are two essential ones, which we shall meet again in the examples&nbsp;:
</p>
<ul>
    <li>
        <strong>The <span class="mot-latin">quilisma</span></strong> — a note drawn as a
        zigzag, light and fleeting, which acts as a springboard to the next note&nbsp;;
        traditionally the preceding note is slightly lengthened.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>The episema</strong> — a small horizontal stroke added above (or beside) a
        neume to indicate that the note is <em>settled on</em>, <em>broadened</em>. It is the
        equivalent of a slight «&nbsp;leaning&nbsp;» of the voice.
    </li>
</ul>

<div class="galerie-neumes">
    <div class="carte-neume">
        <span class="mini-legende">Graduale Triplex</span>
        <img class="zoomable" src="{{ asset('assets/img/neume-quilisma.jpg') }}" alt="A quilisma: a zigzag-ornamented note within an ascending group, here on 'De-' of 'Dei', Graduale Triplex p. 48">
        <span class="mini-legende">Modern transcription</span>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 160 90" role="img" aria-label="Modern transcription: ascending notes with quilisma ornament" class="svg-portee-moderne zoomable">
